# Ostos implementation plan

Status: approved implementation baseline  
Last reconciled: 2026-10-17  
Initial delivery: mobile application and Go API  
Near-future delivery: complete authenticated web application

This document is the ordered implementation authority for Ostos. When an implementation detail here conflicts with `ARCHITECTURE.md`, this plan records the later approved decision and takes precedence until the architecture document is reconciled.

## 1. Establish the product intention

1. Build Ostos as a small, real application for a small userbase.
2. Let a person create an account, verify their email, create or join Families, and collaborate on one shared grocery list per Family.
3. Give every Family exactly one Owner and permit additional Managers and Members.
4. Let authorized Family participants add, edit, reorder, check, uncheck, keep, discard, and purchase grocery-list entries.
5. Treat the current grocery list as the live planning and shopping surface rather than creating a persisted active-trip aggregate.
6. When the current list is completed:
   1. Copy checked items into immutable purchase history.
   2. Remove purchased items from the current list.
   3. Retain unchecked items selected as `keep`, clearing any temporary shopping state.
   4. Delete unchecked items selected as `discard` without placing them in history.
7. Record who added every current-list item and who completed each historical purchase.
8. Allow the completing user to record an optional freeform supermarket name until a normalized supermarket catalog is introduced.
9. Allow actual purchased quantity and total amount paid in cents to be recorded for purchased items.
10. Optimize for correctness, clarity, a tiny operational footprint, and the stated OCI Always Free constraints rather than hypothetical large-scale traffic.
11. Keep the initial product useful without a product catalog, retailer feed, barcode database, or complete supermarket assortment.
12. Preserve a future path toward brands, exact product variants, product images, supermarket branches, retailer offers, and local price comparison without implementing those systems now.

## 2. Fix the current delivery scope

1. Include these capabilities in the first complete delivery:
   1. Self-hosted account registration.
   2. Email verification through AWS SES.
   3. Username/email and password login.
   4. Thirty-minute access JWTs.
   5. Rotating database-backed refresh sessions.
   6. Logout of the current session.
   7. Logout of every session.
   8. Forgotten-password and password-reset flows.
   9. Current-user profile reads and edits.
   10. Family creation, update, deletion, ownership transfer, and membership administration.
   11. Exact-identifier Family invitations.
   12. A maximum of ten Family memberships or owned Families per user.
   13. One shared current grocery list per Family.
   14. Purchase completion with purchased, kept, and discarded outcomes.
   15. Purchase-only shopping history.
   16. A mobile read cache and an explicit offline warning.
   17. Terraform-provisioned OCI and Cloudflare infrastructure.
   18. Ansible-configured AMD Micro virtual machines.
2. Defer these capabilities from the initial delivery:
   1. Product, brand, manufacturer, and exact-variant catalogs.
   2. GTIN or barcode lookup.
   3. Product image upload or moderation.
   4. Normalized supermarket chains and physical branches.
   5. Retailer API integrations.
   6. Public price comparison.
   7. Promotion and loyalty-card modeling.
   8. Push, email, or in-app notification features beyond account verification and password reset.
   9. WebSockets.
   10. Dedicated search services.
   11. Prometheus or a self-hosted metrics stack.
   12. Application-managed database backup jobs and restore workflows.
   13. An offline mutation queue or conflict-resolution protocol.
3. Deliver the React Native application first.
4. Add the complete authenticated website shortly afterward as a separate client over the same API.
5. Do not build public marketing, feature, terms, privacy, or support routes during the planned website phase.
6. Revisit any app-store requirement for a privacy-policy URL before public store distribution, without turning that requirement into a marketing-site project.

## 3. Adopt the final technical stack

1. Use the current supported Go release for the backend.
2. Use Echo v5 for HTTP routing and middleware.
3. Use GORM for MySQL queries, row mapping, and explicit transactions.
4. Never call GORM `AutoMigrate` in application or test startup.
5. Use a separate root Go module for versioned SQL migrations.
6. Use `go-playground/validator/v10` as the common HTTP request-structure validator.
7. Use a maintained JWT library for signing and verification rather than implementing token encoding or cryptography manually.
8. Use Argon2id from maintained cryptographic packages for password hashing.
9. Use a maintained Twitter-style Snowflake implementation for identifiers.
10. Use MySQL HeatWave `MySQL.Free` as the managed transactional database without enabling the analytical accelerator initially.
11. Use Expo and React Native for the first client.
12. Use Expo Router for native routes and deep links.
13. Use TanStack Query for remote state in mobile and the future web client.
14. Use platform secure storage for access and refresh tokens.
15. Use lightweight local persistence for the last successful Family/list snapshot.
16. Use React, Vite, and TanStack Router for the future website.
17. Do not introduce a JavaScript server or SSR runtime.
18. Use Cloudflare DNS, TLS, proxying, and Tunnel for public API ingress.
19. Use OCI Object Storage for Terraform state and any later object-storage requirement.
20. Use AWS SES only for account verification and password-reset email.
21. Use Terraform for OCI, Cloudflare, and infrastructure-level AWS SES resources where practical.
22. Use Ansible for operating-system and service configuration.
23. Run the API and `cloudflared` as native systemd services in production.
24. Use Docker and Compose only for local development, integration tests, and migration tooling.

## 4. Create the repository structure

1. Create this root layout incrementally as each phase begins:

   ```text
   /
     AGENTS.md
     ARCHITECTURE.md
     PLAN.md

     api/
       openapi.yaml

     backend/
       go.mod
       go.sum
       Dockerfile
       compose.yml
       cmd/
         api/
           main.go
       internal/
         accounts/
         families/
         shopping/
         apierrors/
         config/
         platform/
         repository/
           mysql/
         transport/
           http/
             handler/
             middleware/
             request/
             response/
             router/
             validation/

     migrations/
       go.mod
       go.sum
       Dockerfile
       compose.yml
       cmd/
         migrate/
           main.go
       sql/

     mobile/

     web/                    # created only when the website phase begins

     infrastructure/
       terraform/
         bootstrap/
         environments/
           production/
         modules/
       ansible/
         inventories/
         playbooks/
         roles/

     docs/
       adr/
       runbooks/
   ```

2. Organize domain and application behavior by capability rather than by a global service layer:

   ```text
   backend/internal/accounts/
     user.go
     credential.go
     session.go
     repository.go
     service.go
     errors.go

   backend/internal/families/
     family.go
     member.go
     invitation.go
     repository.go
     service.go
     policy.go
     errors.go

   backend/internal/shopping/
     list_item.go
     trip.go
     trip_item.go
     repository.go
     service.go
     policy.go
     errors.go
   ```

3. Keep all concrete MySQL implementations in one explicitly named adapter package for the current project size:

   ```text
   backend/internal/repository/mysql/
     accounts.go
     families.go
     shopping.go
     email_outbox.go
     audit.go
     transactions.go
   ```

4. Define repository interfaces in the capability that consumes them:
   1. `accounts.Repository` belongs to `accounts`.
   2. `families.Repository` belongs to `families`.
   3. `shopping.Repository` belongs to `shopping`.
5. Give concrete constructors explicit names:

   ```go
   accountRepository := mysql.NewAccountRepository(db)
   familyRepository := mysql.NewFamilyRepository(db)
   shoppingRepository := mysql.NewShoppingRepository(db)
   ```

6. Make constructors accept interface dependencies and return concrete implementations:

   ```go
   func NewService(repository Repository, ids IDGenerator, clock Clock) *Service
   ```

7. Do not return an interface merely to conceal a concrete service or repository.
8. Define the smallest service interface at the handler that consumes it when handler isolation or mocking requires one.
9. Inject infrastructure dependencies once through constructors; do not pass repositories into every business method.
10. Pass `context.Context` and typed application input values into service methods.
11. Keep the package dependency direction acyclic:
    1. Capability packages know their own entities, interfaces, and policies.
    2. MySQL adapters import capability packages to implement their interfaces.
    3. HTTP transport imports capability types and service-facing interfaces.
    4. Capability packages never import MySQL or Echo.
    5. The application composition root imports every concrete package and wires the graph.
12. Keep GORM tags on persistent capability entities as approved, while keeping HTTP validation and JSON binding on separate request DTOs.
13. Do not serialize GORM entities directly into HTTP responses.

## 5. Establish identifiers, timestamps, JSON, quantities, and money conventions

1. Give every platform entity a positive Twitter-style Snowflake primary key stored as MySQL `BIGINT` and represented in Go through a dedicated identifier type or validated `int64`.
2. Fix the Snowflake epoch before creating the first persistent production entity and never change it afterward.
3. Assign a stable Snowflake node ID from `0` through `1023` to every API process.
4. Configure the initial API VM with a stable value such as `SNOWFLAKE_NODE_ID=1`.
5. Never derive a node ID from a public IP address or another ephemeral value.
6. Fail application startup when the node ID is invalid or unavailable.
7. Return every entity identifier as a decimal JSON string to avoid JavaScript integer overflow:

   ```json
   {
     "id": "241895317264384000",
     "family_id": "241895500112232448"
   }
   ```

8. Parse every incoming path/body identifier as a positive signed 63-bit decimal string.
9. Reject zero, negative, malformed, and overflowed identifiers with stable errors.
10. Use `snake_case` for every JSON property.
11. Use UTC internally and RFC 3339 timestamps in JSON.
12. Store database timestamps with microsecond precision where supported.
13. Include `created_at` and `updated_at` on mutable entities, including `users`.
14. Represent grocery quantities as JSON numbers with at most three decimal places.
15. Decode quantity tokens through `json.Number` or a custom fixed-decimal type rather than binding through `float64`.
16. Store quantities in MySQL `DECIMAL(12,3)`.
17. Use an initial unit enumeration of `each`, `g`, `kg`, `ml`, and `l`.
18. Store all paid amounts as nonnegative integer cents in `BIGINT` columns such as `total_cents`.
19. Never use floating point for money.
20. Treat BRL as the documented deployment-level currency for the one-city product and omit per-row currency until multi-currency support becomes a real requirement.

## 6. Implement request binding and validation consistently

1. Install one `go-playground/validator/v10` instance during application composition.
2. Register it through Echo's validator integration.
3. Put validation tags on every HTTP request DTO, including but not limited to:
   1. `UserCreateRequest`.
   2. `UserUpdateRequest`.
   3. `PasswordChangeRequest`.
   4. `LoginRequest`.
   5. `RefreshRequest` when the token is supplied in JSON.
   6. `ForgotPasswordRequest`.
   7. `ResetPasswordRequest`.
   8. `FamilyCreateRequest`.
   9. `FamilyUpdateRequest`.
   10. `FamilyInvitationCreateRequest`.
   11. `FamilyMemberUpdateRequest`.
   12. `FamilyOwnershipTransferRequest`.
   13. `ShoppingListItemCreateRequest`.
   14. `ShoppingListItemUpdateRequest`.
   15. `ShoppingListOrderRequest`.
   16. `ShoppingListCompleteRequest`.
4. Use built-in validation tags for simple structural requirements such as `required`, `min`, `max`, `email`, `oneof`, `gt`, and `omitempty`.
5. Register custom validator tags for reusable formats that built-in tags cannot express cleanly.
6. Register struct-level validators for mutually exclusive fields and cross-field request relationships.
7. Validate usernames with this contract:
   1. Length from 2 through 32 ASCII characters.
   2. Lowercase `a` through `z`.
   3. Digits `0` through `9`.
   4. Hyphen `-`.
   5. Underscore `_`.
   6. No spaces or other characters.
8. Implement the username character check as a registered custom validator rather than a scattered regex.
9. Define the DTO tag as:

   ```go
   Username string `json:"username" validate:"required,min=2,max=32,username"`
   ```

10. Collect every field failure in one validation pass and return all failed fields.
11. Keep uniqueness, authorization, membership limits, ownership, and transaction invariants in services and database constraints rather than pretending struct tags can enforce them.
12. Bind into request DTOs only; never bind client JSON directly into GORM entities.

## 7. Standardize the API error contract

1. Return JSON failures through one envelope:

   ```json
   {
     "errors": {
       "code": "INVALID_FORM_BODY",
       "message": "One or more fields are invalid",
       "fields": {
         "email": "Enter a valid email address",
         "username": "Use 2-32 lowercase letters, digits, hyphens, or underscores"
       }
     }
   }
   ```

2. Always include a stable `errors.code` in a JSON error.
3. Let clients branch on `errors.code`, never on human-readable text.
4. Include `message` when a request-level explanation helps.
5. Include `fields` only after syntactically valid JSON was successfully bound and individual fields failed validation.
6. Return malformed JSON without a `fields` object:

   ```json
   {
     "errors": {
       "code": "MALFORMED_JSON",
       "message": "The request body must contain valid JSON"
     }
   }
   ```

7. Map expected classes consistently:
   1. `400 Bad Request` for malformed semantics outside field validation and ineligible invitees.
   2. `401 Unauthorized` for missing, invalid, or expired authentication.
   3. `403 Forbidden` for authenticated callers without permission and accounts awaiting email verification when required.
   4. `404 Not Found` for inaccessible or unknown resources when revealing their existence is safe.
   5. `409 Conflict` for unique conflicts, membership limits, invalid ownership transitions, and incompatible current state.
   6. `413 Content Too Large` for bounded-body violations.
   7. `415 Unsupported Media Type` for unsupported content types.
   8. `422 Unprocessable Content` for parsed request-field validation failures.
   9. `429 Too Many Requests` for rate limits.
   10. `500 Internal Server Error` for unexpected application or infrastructure failure.
   11. `503 Service Unavailable` when a required dependency is unavailable.
8. Return generic internal messages and never expose SQL, stack traces, credentials, token material, or provider response internals.
9. Use `204 No Content` for successful commands that intentionally return no representation.
10. Return direct resource representations rather than unnecessary wrapper keys.

## 8. Build the migration module before repositories

1. Initialize `migrations` as an independent Go module.
2. Keep its executable at `migrations/cmd/migrate/main.go` without an unnecessary internal runner abstraction.
3. Make the migration executable:
   1. Load configuration.
   2. Open MySQL with bounded timeouts.
   3. Invoke the selected versioned migration library.
   4. Emit safe structured logs.
   5. Exit nonzero on any failure.
4. Store ordered SQL files under `migrations/sql`.
5. Give the migration process schema-administration credentials that the API never receives.
6. Make migrations forward-oriented and compatible with an old and new application binary during deployment transitions.
7. Do not assume a destructive schema downgrade is safe.
8. Create separate Docker and Compose files for the migration tool's local and CI execution.
9. Create an integration-test command that starts real MySQL, applies every migration from empty, and inspects constraints and indexes.
10. Verify GORM entity mappings against the migrated schema without invoking automatic schema changes.

## 9. Create the initial database schema

1. Create `users` with:
   1. `id BIGINT` primary key.
   2. `email VARCHAR(320)` unique and stored in the application's canonical form.
   3. `username VARCHAR(32)` unique and stored exactly as validated lowercase ASCII.
   4. `display_name VARCHAR(80)`.
   5. `email_verified_at DATETIME(6)` nullable.
   6. `created_at DATETIME(6)`.
   7. `updated_at DATETIME(6)`.
   8. `deleted_at DATETIME(6)` nullable when soft account deletion is retained.
2. Do not create duplicate `email_normalized` or `username_normalized` columns.
3. Canonicalize email consistently before validation/storage and enforce uniqueness on the stored value.
4. Create `user_credentials` with:
   1. Snowflake `id`.
   2. Unique `user_id` foreign key.
   3. Argon2id `password_hash`.
   4. `password_changed_at`.
   5. `created_at` and `updated_at`.
5. Create `auth_sessions` with:
   1. Snowflake `id` used as the JWT session claim.
   2. `user_id` foreign key.
   3. Unique `refresh_token_hash`.
   4. Optional bounded `device_name`.
   5. `created_at`.
   6. `last_used_at`.
   7. `expires_at`.
   8. Nullable `revoked_at`.
   9. Nullable `replaced_by_session_id` for refresh rotation.
6. Create `account_tokens` with:
   1. Snowflake `id`.
   2. `user_id` foreign key.
   3. `purpose` limited to `verify_email` or `reset_password`.
   4. Unique token hash rather than the raw bearer token.
   5. `expires_at`.
   6. Nullable `used_at`.
   7. `created_at`.
7. Create `families` with:
   1. Snowflake `id`.
   2. `name VARCHAR(80)`.
   3. Non-null `owner_id` foreign key to `users`.
   4. `created_at` and `updated_at`.
   5. Optional `deleted_at` when Family deletion is recoverable within the database.
8. Do not create a Family revision column.
9. Do not create an Owner membership row; `families.owner_id` is the authoritative Owner relationship.
10. Create `family_members` with:
    1. Snowflake `id`.
    2. `family_id` foreign key.
    3. `user_id` foreign key.
    4. `role` limited to `manager` or `member`.
    5. `created_at` and `updated_at`.
    6. Unique `(family_id, user_id)`.
    7. An index beginning with `user_id` for membership listing and limits.
11. Create `family_invitations` as pending invitations only, with:
    1. Snowflake `id`.
    2. `family_id` foreign key.
    3. `invited_user_id` foreign key.
    4. `invited_by_user_id` foreign key.
    5. `expires_at`.
    6. `created_at`.
    7. Unique `(family_id, invited_user_id)`.
12. Do not store an invitation role; successful acceptance always creates a Member.
13. Do not store invitation status, response time, or a generated pending guard.
14. Delete invitation rows when accepted, declined, cancelled, or cleaned up after expiry.
15. Create `shopping_list_items` with:
    1. Snowflake `id`.
    2. `family_id` foreign key.
    3. Required freeform `name VARCHAR(160)`.
    4. `quantity DECIMAL(12,3)` with a positive default of one.
    5. `unit` limited to the approved unit set.
    6. Nullable `note VARCHAR(500)`.
    7. `is_checked BOOLEAN`.
    8. Nullable `purchased_quantity DECIMAL(12,3)` for current shopping input.
    9. Nullable nonnegative `total_cents BIGINT` for current shopping input.
    10. `position BIGINT` for stable manual order.
    11. `added_by_id` foreign key to `users`.
    12. `updated_by_id` foreign key to `users`.
    13. `created_at` and `updated_at`.
16. Do not add an optimistic item-version column; concurrent updates intentionally use last database commit wins.
17. Create `shopping_trips` only as completed purchase-history headers, with:
    1. Snowflake `id`.
    2. `family_id` foreign key.
    3. Nullable bounded `supermarket_name`.
    4. `completed_by_id` foreign key to `users`.
    5. `completed_at`.
    6. `created_at`.
18. Do not add trip status, start time, starter, cancellation time, or active-trip constraints.
19. Create a trip row only when at least one item was purchased.
20. Create `shopping_trip_items` as immutable purchased-item snapshots, with:
    1. Snowflake `id`.
    2. `trip_id` foreign key.
    3. Non-null `source_item_id` containing the originating current-list ID.
    4. Snapshotted `name`.
    5. Snapshotted requested `quantity` and `unit`.
    6. Snapshotted nullable `note`.
    7. `added_by_id` identifying who originally added the list entry.
    8. Nullable actual `purchased_quantity`.
    9. Nullable nonnegative `total_cents`.
    10. `created_at`.
21. Do not require a foreign key from `source_item_id` to the current list because the source row is deleted after a purchase and history must remain immutable.
22. Create `email_outbox` solely for verification and password-reset email, with:
    1. Snowflake `id`.
    2. `user_id`.
    3. `template` limited to approved account-email templates.
    4. Recipient email.
    5. Safe non-secret template data.
    6. An encrypted short-lived token payload required to construct the verification or reset link.
    7. Delivery state.
    8. Attempt count.
    9. Next attempt time.
    10. Nullable sent time.
    11. `created_at` and `updated_at`.
23. Encrypt the outbox token payload with an application key held outside the database, clear it after successful delivery, and retain only the account-token hash needed for single-use verification.
24. Create `audit_events` for ownership, role, invitation, Family deletion, account security, and history-correction actions, with:
    1. Snowflake `id`.
    2. Nullable actor user ID for system actions.
    3. Nullable Family ID.
    4. Stable action code.
    5. Target type and nullable target ID.
    6. Safe JSON metadata.
    7. Request ID.
    8. `created_at`.
25. Do not audit every grocery-list keystroke.
26. Accept the migration library's own schema-history table as infrastructure metadata that does not require a Snowflake identifier.

## 10. Implement self-hosted authentication

1. Create users through `POST /v1/users`, not an authentication-prefixed registration route.
2. Validate the entire request and return every invalid field.
3. In the registration transaction:
   1. Generate the User and credential Snowflake IDs.
   2. Canonicalize email.
   3. Validate the username exactly as received.
   4. Hash the password with configured Argon2id parameters.
   5. Insert the User and credential.
   6. Create a hashed email-verification token record.
   7. Insert an email-outbox job.
4. Return `201 Created`, a `Location` header, and the direct User representation:

   ```json
   {
     "id": "241895317264384000",
     "email": "user@example.com",
     "username": "leonardo",
     "display_name": "Leonardo",
     "email_verified": false,
     "created_at": "2026-10-17T12:00:00Z",
     "updated_at": "2026-10-17T12:00:00Z"
   }
   ```

5. Never include a redundant top-level `user` wrapper.
6. Require successful email verification before ordinary login.
7. Make verification and reset tokens random, single-use, expiring, and stored only as hashes.
8. Use AWS SES for delivery through a least-privilege IAM identity restricted to the verified sending identity.
9. Configure SES domain verification, DKIM, SPF, and DMARC through the owned domain and Cloudflare DNS.
10. Account for the SES sandbox and complete production-access approval before inviting arbitrary recipient addresses.
11. Issue a signed access JWT valid for 30 minutes after successful login or refresh.
12. Put only stable authentication claims in the JWT:
    1. Subject User ID as a decimal string.
    2. Session ID as a decimal string.
    3. Issuer.
    4. Audience.
    5. Issued-at time.
    6. Expiry time.
    7. Unique token ID.
13. Do not put Family IDs, ownership, membership roles, or permissions in JWT claims.
14. Generate a high-entropy opaque refresh token for each session.
15. Store only its hash in `auth_sessions`.
16. Rotate the refresh token on every successful refresh and make reuse of a replaced token revoke the affected session chain.
17. Make normal logout revoke only the current refresh session.
18. Make logout-all and password reset revoke every refresh session for the User.
19. Do not persist access JWTs or maintain an access-token denylist.
20. Accept that an already-issued access JWT may remain usable for at most 30 minutes after logout.
21. Check current Family ownership or membership in MySQL for every Family-scoped operation, so removing a user takes effect immediately despite JWT lifetime.
22. Store mobile tokens only in operating-system secure storage, never in the list cache.
23. Rate-limit registration, login, verification resend, refresh, and password-reset requests without adding a separate cache service.

## 11. Implement Family ownership, roles, and invitations

1. Represent the Owner solely through `families.owner_id`.
2. Represent non-owner participants in `family_members` with `manager` or `member`.
3. Return the Owner as part of the member-list API even though the Owner does not have a `family_members` row.
4. Apply this permission matrix:
   1. Owner may view and edit the Family and grocery list.
   2. Owner may invite, remove, promote, and demote Managers and Members.
   3. Owner may transfer ownership and delete the Family.
   4. Manager may view and edit the Family and grocery list.
   5. Manager may invite and remove Members.
   6. Manager may not change the Owner or another Manager.
   7. Member may view and edit the shared grocery list.
   8. Member may not manage membership or Family settings.
5. Do not expose user search, suggestions, autocomplete, or a general user lookup endpoint.
6. Accept an invitation target through exactly one of:
   1. Exact validated username.
   2. Exact Snowflake User ID string.
7. Resolve the identifier privately and never return the target user's profile from invitation creation.
8. Return `204 No Content` after successful invitation creation.
9. Return the same public `INVITEE_NOT_ELIGIBLE` response for nonexistent, already participating, already invited, over-limit, and otherwise ineligible targets where distinction would enable account enumeration.
10. Apply per-caller and per-target invitation rate limits.
11. Accepting an invitation always creates a `member` role.
12. Promote a Member later through the membership update route.
13. Enforce a maximum of ten Families per User, counting both:
    1. Families where `families.owner_id` equals the User.
    2. Rows in `family_members` for the User.
14. Enforce the limit inside a transaction that locks the User row before counting and inserting.
15. Recheck the limit at invitation acceptance even if it was checked at invitation creation.
16. Create a Family and establish its Owner in one transaction.
17. Transfer ownership in one transaction:
    1. Lock the Family.
    2. Verify `new_owner_id` currently identifies a Manager or Member in the Family.
    3. Verify ownership and the ten-Family rule.
    4. Delete the new Owner's membership row.
    5. Insert the old Owner as a Manager.
    6. Update `families.owner_id`.
    7. Record an audit event.
    8. Commit.
18. Prevent the Owner from leaving or being removed before ownership transfer.
19. Prevent a User from appearing simultaneously as `owner_id` and in `family_members` through service logic and integration tests.
20. Delete pending invitation rows on acceptance, decline, cancellation, or expiry cleanup.

## 12. Implement the current grocery list

1. Treat a Family's current list as the set of rows in `shopping_list_items`; do not create a redundant `shopping_lists` table.
2. Require a freeform item name so the application remains usable without a product catalog.
3. Allow optional notes and supported desired quantity/unit values.
4. Record `added_by_id` at creation and never change it.
5. Record `updated_by_id` on each edit.
6. Assign sparse sortable positions so most reorder operations do not require rewriting every row.
7. Add, edit, check, uncheck, reorder, and delete entries through explicit state assignments.
8. Do not design a retry-unsafe toggle endpoint.
9. Permit a checked item to hold nullable actual purchased quantity and total cents before completion.
10. Clear temporary purchased quantity and cents when an item is unchecked unless the UI explicitly confirms retaining them.
11. Use last-write-wins for concurrent item changes based on database commit order.
12. Do not expose record versions or conditional-write headers.
13. Return `404 LIST_ITEM_NOT_FOUND` when an update arrives after deletion.
14. Scope every list query by both `family_id` and the item identifier.
15. Verify the actor's current Family role for every operation.

## 13. Implement completion and purchase-only history

1. Complete the current list through one command endpoint rather than starting, cancelling, or maintaining a persisted active trip.
2. Accept:
   1. Optional `supermarket_name`.
   2. `default_unchecked_action` of `keep` or `discard`.
   3. Optional per-item overrides for unchecked entries.
3. Derive `completed_by_id` from the authenticated User rather than accepting it in the request body.
4. In one MySQL transaction:
   1. Lock the Family and current list rows in stable order.
   2. Recheck authorization.
   3. Partition checked items as purchased.
   4. Partition unchecked items into keep or discard according to the default and overrides.
   5. If at least one item was purchased, create one `shopping_trips` history header.
   6. Copy every purchased item into `shopping_trip_items` as an immutable snapshot.
   7. Delete purchased source items from the current list.
   8. Retain kept items while setting `is_checked=false` and clearing temporary purchase data.
   9. Delete discarded items without placing them in history.
   10. Record the completion audit event where appropriate.
   11. Commit atomically.
5. If no item was purchased, apply keep/discard changes without creating an empty history header.
6. Never place kept or discarded entries in purchase history.
7. Never mutate a historical item because its current-list source or future catalog data changed.
8. Allow corrections to purchase history only through a later explicit, authorized, audited feature; do not silently reuse ordinary list editing.

## 14. Publish the initial HTTP routes

1. Use `/v1` as the business API prefix.
2. Keep the operational health endpoint outside the versioned resource API:

   ```text
   GET /health
   ```

3. Make `/health` return:
   1. `200` when the process and required database are ready.
   2. `503` when the process responds but the required database is unavailable.
   3. No credentials, hostnames, SQL, or internal topology details.
4. Publish account creation and current-user resources:

   ```text
   POST   /v1/users
   GET    /v1/users/me
   PATCH  /v1/users/me
   PUT    /v1/users/me/password
   DELETE /v1/users/me
   ```

5. Do not publish a general `GET /v1/users/{user_id}` route.
6. Publish authentication commands:

   ```text
   POST /v1/auth/login
   POST /v1/auth/refresh
   POST /v1/auth/logout
   POST /v1/auth/logout-all
   POST /v1/auth/verify-email
   POST /v1/auth/resend-verification
   POST /v1/auth/forgot-password
   POST /v1/auth/reset-password
   ```

7. Publish Family resources:

   ```text
   GET    /v1/families
   POST   /v1/families
   GET    /v1/families/{family_id}
   PATCH  /v1/families/{family_id}
   DELETE /v1/families/{family_id}
   ```

8. Make `GET /v1/families` return authorized Family summaries in one request rather than putting only Family IDs in the current-user response and causing one request per Family.
9. Publish membership resources and ownership transfer:

   ```text
   GET    /v1/families/{family_id}/members
   PATCH  /v1/families/{family_id}/members/{member_id}
   DELETE /v1/families/{family_id}/members/{member_id}
   POST   /v1/families/{family_id}/leave
   POST   /v1/families/{family_id}/transfer-ownership
   ```

10. Accept this transfer payload, with `new_owner_id` documented as a User ID:

    ```json
    {
      "new_owner_id": "241895615438458880"
    }
    ```

11. Publish received- and Family-invitation resources:

    ```text
    GET    /v1/invitations
    POST   /v1/invitations/{invitation_id}/accept
    POST   /v1/invitations/{invitation_id}/decline

    GET    /v1/families/{family_id}/invitations
    POST   /v1/families/{family_id}/invitations
    DELETE /v1/families/{family_id}/invitations/{invitation_id}
    ```

12. Accept exactly one invitation identifier:

    ```json
    {
      "username": "exact_username"
    }
    ```

    or:

    ```json
    {
      "user_id": "241895317264384000"
    }
    ```

13. Publish current-list resources:

    ```text
    GET    /v1/families/{family_id}/shopping-list
    POST   /v1/families/{family_id}/shopping-list/items
    PATCH  /v1/families/{family_id}/shopping-list/items/{item_id}
    DELETE /v1/families/{family_id}/shopping-list/items/{item_id}
    PUT    /v1/families/{family_id}/shopping-list/items/order
    POST   /v1/families/{family_id}/shopping-list/complete
    ```

14. Use a completion request shaped like:

    ```json
    {
      "supermarket_name": "Market downtown",
      "default_unchecked_action": "keep",
      "unchecked_item_overrides": [
        {
          "item_id": "241896015117107200",
          "action": "discard"
        }
      ]
    }
    ```

15. Publish purchase-history reads:

    ```text
    GET /v1/families/{family_id}/shopping-trips
    GET /v1/families/{family_id}/shopping-trips/{trip_id}
    ```

16. Do not publish start-trip, active-trip, cancel-trip, product, catalog, offer, supermarket-search, notification, or user-search routes in the initial API.

## 15. Define the API contract and transport behavior

1. Make `api/openapi.yaml` the source of truth for route paths, methods, DTOs, responses, and error codes.
2. Generate server transport types or interfaces where the chosen Echo tooling supports them cleanly.
3. Generate the TypeScript API client consumed by mobile and the future website.
4. Keep generated transport types out of capability entities.
5. Map request DTOs into typed application inputs explicitly.
6. Map application results into response DTOs explicitly.
7. Apply bounded request-body sizes before JSON binding.
8. Require `application/json` for JSON-body routes.
9. Reject unknown JSON fields for mutation requests unless a deliberate compatibility policy says otherwise.
10. Propagate one request ID through Cloudflare, Echo, service logs, audit events, and error logs.
11. Set authenticated responses to `Cache-Control: private, no-store`.
12. Return collection results in stable envelopes such as:

    ```json
    {
      "families": []
    }
    ```

13. Return individual resources directly without an unnecessary type wrapper.
14. Use opaque cursor pagination for potentially growing history collections.
15. Keep the current Family list unpaginated while its expected size remains small and measured response size remains safe.

## 16. Build the React Native application

1. Create these authentication screens:
   1. Login.
   2. Registration.
   3. Email verification.
   4. Resend verification.
   5. Forgotten password.
   6. Reset password.
2. Create these authenticated screens:
   1. Family list.
   2. Create Family.
   3. Current Family grocery list.
   4. Purchase history list.
   5. Purchase-history detail.
   6. Family members.
   7. Received invitations.
   8. Family invitations.
   9. Family settings.
   10. Account profile.
   11. Change password.
   12. Session/logout controls.
3. Use secure storage for access and refresh tokens.
4. Refresh the access JWT through the rotating refresh session when the access JWT expires or shortly before expiry.
5. If refresh fails because the session is expired or revoked, clear secure credentials and return to login.
6. Cache the last successful Family/list representation locally with:
   1. Family ID.
   2. Current item representation.
   3. `last_checked_at` from the successful fetch time.
7. Render cached data immediately while attempting an online refresh.
8. When offline or the API is unreachable:
   1. Keep showing the cached list.
   2. Show `Offline · last checked <local time>`.
   3. Place the warning at the upper-left edge of the grocery-list button in the bottom navigation/footer as requested.
   4. Make it visually clear that the displayed data may be stale.
   5. Disable authoritative mutations or fail them before changing the displayed server state.
9. Do not queue offline mutations in the initial implementation.
10. Trigger refresh on app launch, foreground, reconnect, pull-to-refresh, and successful mutation.
11. Keep screen and router code native rather than sharing complete UI with the future DOM application.
12. Share generated API types, API client behavior, validation-derived presentation rules, query keys, money helpers, quantity helpers, translation keys, and design tokens where valuable.
13. Do not add a notification settings screen or request notification permissions.

## 17. Plan the near-future authenticated website

1. Create the `web` workspace only after the initial API and mobile critical path are stable.
2. Use React, Vite, TanStack Router, TanStack Query, strict TypeScript, and the generated API client.
3. Enable route-level code splitting and keep account, Family, list, and history screens in separate route chunks where useful.
4. Implement these browser routes:

   ```text
   /
   /login
   /register
   /verify-email
   /forgot-password
   /reset-password

   /app
   /app/families
   /app/families/new
   /app/families/{family_id}/list
   /app/families/{family_id}/history
   /app/families/{family_id}/history/{trip_id}
   /app/families/{family_id}/members
   /app/families/{family_id}/invitations
   /app/families/{family_id}/settings
   /app/account
   ```

5. Make `/` inspect the local authentication state and redirect:
   1. Authenticated users to `/app`.
   2. Unauthenticated users to `/login`.
6. Make `/app` redirect to the last active Family list or the Family chooser.
7. Deploy the static Vite build on Cloudflare Pages.
8. Configure Pages rewrites for dynamic application routes.
9. Do not add SSR, server actions, a frontend backend, or duplicated authorization logic.
10. Make the website fully functional for the current account, Family, membership, list, completion, and history scope rather than a reduced landing page.

## 18. Provision Terraform state safely

1. Create a small `infra/terraform/bootstrap` stack that creates the OCI Object Storage bucket used for Terraform state.
2. Keep bootstrap state separate from the main production stack.
3. Enable state-bucket versioning because Terraform state is infrastructure control data, not an application-data backup feature.
4. Configure the production root with:

   ```hcl
   terraform {
     backend "oci" {}
   }
   ```

5. Commit `backend.oci.tfbackend.example` containing placeholders only.
6. Ignore the real `backend.production.tfbackend` file.
7. Put bucket, namespace, region, state key, and a non-secret OCI profile name in the backend configuration.
8. Keep API private keys, fingerprints where avoidable, and other credentials in OCI configuration, environment variables, or CI secrets rather than committed files.
9. Initialize production with:

   ```text
   terraform init -backend-config=backend.production.tfbackend
   ```

10. Use remote state locking supplied by the OCI backend.
11. Restrict state-bucket IAM to the identities that plan or apply infrastructure.

## 19. Provision the OCI network and AMD Micro compute

1. Create one VCN, initially using a layout such as:
   1. VCN `10.0.0.0/16`.
   2. Public VM subnet `10.0.1.0/24`.
   3. Private MySQL subnet `10.0.2.0/24`.
2. Create an Internet Gateway and public-subnet route for VM outbound access.
3. Do not create a potentially billable NAT Gateway for the initial topology.
4. Query OCI availability domains at the production root through `oci_identity_availability_domains`.
5. Select the AD using `availability_domain_index` from `terraform.tfvars` rather than hard-coding a tenancy-specific name.
6. Validate the index against the returned AD list and support single-AD regions.
7. Recognize that the AD data source lists domains but cannot guarantee E2 Micro capacity.
8. If OCI reports capacity failure, change the configured AD index and apply again.
9. Create only `VM.Standard.E2.1.Micro` application instances in the approved compute module.
10. Assign each VM:
    1. A stable private IPv4.
    2. A public IPv4.
    3. The compute NSG.
    4. The MySQL-client NSG when the VM is authorized to access MySQL.
    5. An SSH public key.
    6. A stable Snowflake node ID delivered through configuration management.
11. Expose an `admin_ipv4_cidr` variable in `terraform.tfvars.example`.
12. Recommend a single-host value such as `203.0.113.10/32` for administrator access.
13. Permit inbound TCP 22 only from `admin_ipv4_cidr`.
14. Do not permit public inbound TCP 80, 443, 8080, or MySQL.
15. Bind Echo to `127.0.0.1:8080`.
16. Use outbound-only Cloudflare Tunnel connectivity from `cloudflared` to publish the API domain.
17. Create a dedicated MySQL-client NSG and attach it to every authorized VM VNIC.
18. Configure the MySQL NSG to permit stateful TCP 3306 from the MySQL-client NSG, not from a public CIDR and not from the entire subnet.
19. Give the MySQL DB system a private endpoint only.
20. Require TLS and a least-privilege API database user.
21. Do not expose MySQL directly to the administrator's home IPv4.
22. Allow additional VMs to access MySQL by attaching the approved client NSG rather than broadening the database rule.
23. Use Terraform outputs for VM public IPs, private IPs, and safe operational identifiers.
24. Keep real `terraform.tfvars` ignored and commit only `terraform.tfvars.example`.
25. Put no database, AWS, Cloudflare, tunnel, or private-key secret in committed tfvars.

## 20. Provision Cloudflare, SES, and storage boundaries

1. Use the purchased domain with hostnames such as:
   1. `api.example.com` for Cloudflare Tunnel to the Go API.
   2. `example.com` for the future web application.
   3. `www.example.com` as an optional redirect to the apex when the website exists.
2. Create a named Cloudflare Tunnel and DNS record for the API.
3. Configure the tunnel origin as `http://127.0.0.1:8080`.
4. Disable caching for authenticated API responses.
5. Use Cloudflare edge protections and bounded application rate limits without moving business authorization to Cloudflare.
6. Create or verify the AWS SES domain identity.
7. Publish SES DKIM, SPF, and DMARC DNS records through the Cloudflare Terraform provider where supported.
8. Restrict AWS credentials to the minimum SES send actions and verified identity.
9. Store AWS runtime credentials outside Terraform source and outside application logs.
10. Use OCI Object Storage rather than a Cloudflare media store.
11. Do not provision a product-media bucket until product media is actually implemented.
12. Keep GCP regional object storage as a documented alternative, not a simultaneously provisioned dependency.

## 21. Configure the VM with Ansible

1. Use a supported minimal Linux distribution compatible with E2 Micro and the selected OCI image.
2. Create a locked-down non-root `ostos` service user.
3. Configure the host firewall to align with NSG intent.
4. Install and configure `cloudflared` as a systemd service.
5. Install the prebuilt AMD64 Go API binary into versioned release directories.
6. Store runtime configuration in root-owned files unreadable by ordinary users.
7. Create an `ostos-api.service` systemd unit with:
   1. Restart policy.
   2. Resource and file-system hardening that fits the application.
   3. Environment file reference.
   4. Bounded startup and shutdown times.
8. Switch deployments through an atomic `current` symlink.
9. Retain the immediately previous binary release for application rollback.
10. Configure bounded journald storage suitable for a one-gigabyte VM.
11. Do not compile application artifacts on the VM.
12. Do not install a production container engine, reverse proxy, metrics server, search server, or extra database.
13. Verify Ansible convergence by running it twice and confirming the second run produces no unintended changes.

## 22. Implement security and privacy controls

1. Verify JWT signature, algorithm, issuer, audience, expiry, and not-before constraints.
2. Keep JWT signing keys outside source control and support controlled key rotation.
3. Check Family ownership or membership from MySQL for every Family-scoped operation.
4. Require recent password confirmation or equivalent recent authentication before password change, account deletion, or sensitive session revocation where appropriate.
5. Use exact allowed CORS origins; never combine credentialed requests with a wildcard origin.
6. Use parameterized GORM queries and explicitly scoped conditions.
7. Set context deadlines on HTTP, MySQL, SES, and Cloudflare-facing operations.
8. Configure a small MySQL connection pool appropriate for E2 Micro and the free database.
9. Trust Cloudflare client-IP headers only because direct public application ingress is closed, and configure Echo trusted-proxy behavior explicitly.
10. Hash passwords, refresh tokens, verification tokens, and reset tokens with the algorithm appropriate to each secret type.
11. Never log passwords, JWTs, refresh tokens, invitation request bodies containing exact identifiers, verification/reset tokens, AWS credentials, database credentials, or Terraform state.
12. Audit ownership, role, invitation, Family deletion, password/security, and history-correction events.
13. Collect only account and grocery-list data necessary for the application.
14. Do not collect continuous user location.
15. Clear cached Family data and secure credentials on logout.
16. Implement account deletion constraints so an Owner must transfer or delete owned Families first.
17. Document that the initial application has no recovery guarantee for lost or corrupted database data.
18. Leave any no-cost provider-managed database protection enabled when available, but do not claim it as an application recovery design.
19. Protect MySQL and Terraform-managed critical resources from accidental destruction through reviewed lifecycle controls where practical.

## 23. Add lightweight observability and operations

1. Use Go `slog` structured JSON logs.
2. Include safe fields such as:
   1. Request ID.
   2. Method.
   3. Route template rather than raw secret-bearing URL.
   4. Status code.
   5. Duration.
   6. Safe User ID when authenticated.
   7. Stable error code.
3. Keep `/health` inexpensive and bounded.
4. Use OCI instance metrics and Cloudflare request analytics already available within the selected services.
5. Configure alerts only for immediately useful conditions such as VM unreachability, disk pressure, repeated process restart, and health failure.
6. Use systemd and journald for service status and logs.
7. Write runbooks for:
   1. Deployment.
   2. Binary rollback.
   3. Lost VM recreation.
   4. Database connection failure.
   5. Leaked JWT signing key.
   6. Leaked refresh token.
   7. Compromised SES credentials.
   8. Ownership recovery through controlled database administration.
8. Do not add a standalone metrics stack until measured operational needs justify it.

## 24. Establish CI and delivery gates

1. Run backend gates:
   1. `gofmt` verification.
   2. `go vet`.
   3. Selected static analysis.
   4. Dependency vulnerability scanning.
   5. Unit tests.
   6. Race tests where compatible with the suite.
   7. Real-MySQL integration tests.
2. Run migration gates:
   1. Build the independent migration module.
   2. Apply every migration to an empty real MySQL instance.
   3. Verify expected schema constraints.
   4. Run GORM repository compatibility tests.
3. Run API-contract gates:
   1. Lint OpenAPI.
   2. Detect breaking contract changes.
   3. Regenerate server/client types.
   4. Fail when generated files differ from committed output.
4. Run mobile gates:
   1. Formatting and linting.
   2. Strict TypeScript checking.
   3. Unit and component tests.
   4. Build verification.
   5. Critical-device flows.
5. Run future web gates only after the web phase begins:
   1. Formatting and linting.
   2. Strict type checking.
   3. Unit/component tests.
   4. Production build.
   5. Playwright critical journeys.
   6. Route-refresh verification.
6. Run infrastructure gates:
   1. `terraform fmt -check`.
   2. `terraform validate`.
   3. Reviewed saved production plan.
   4. Checks that reject unapproved compute shapes and public database access.
   5. Ansible syntax and lint checks.
   6. Repeated Ansible convergence test against an ephemeral host where practical.
7. Build the Linux AMD64 API artifact in CI, never on the production VM.
8. Use this deployment order:
   1. Review and apply Terraform when infrastructure changed.
   2. Run Ansible when host state changed.
   3. Apply forward database migrations.
   4. Upload the versioned API artifact.
   5. Atomically switch the active binary.
   6. Restart the API.
   7. Run health and external smoke tests.
   8. Roll back the binary when the application fails while leaving forward-compatible schema changes intact.

## 25. Test the critical backend paths exhaustively

1. Test validation behavior:
   1. Every request DTO invokes `go-playground/validator`.
   2. Every invalid field is returned together.
   3. Malformed JSON returns code and message without fields.
   4. Username length boundaries of 1, 2, 32, and 33.
   5. Allowed lowercase letters, digits, hyphen, and underscore.
   6. Rejection of uppercase, spaces, Unicode, and punctuation outside the approved set.
2. Test account behavior:
   1. Canonical email uniqueness.
   2. Username uniqueness under concurrent registration.
   3. Argon2id hashing and verification.
   4. Verification expiry, single use, and resend.
   5. Login rejection before verification.
   6. Generic invalid-credential errors.
   7. Password-reset expiry and single use.
   8. Password reset revokes all refresh sessions.
3. Test JWT and refresh behavior:
   1. Required claims and 30-minute expiry.
   2. Algorithm, issuer, audience, signature, expiry, and not-before rejection.
   3. Refresh-token hash storage.
   4. Rotation replaces the previous token.
   5. Reuse detection revokes the affected session chain.
   6. Current-session logout.
   7. All-session logout.
   8. No Family roles present in JWT claims.
4. Test Family invariants:
   1. Family creation establishes one Owner.
   2. Owner appears in the members response without a membership row.
   3. Owner cannot leave or be removed.
   4. Manager cannot alter Owner or Manager roles.
   5. Member cannot manage hierarchy.
   6. Ownership transfer removes the new Owner's membership and inserts the old Owner as Manager.
   7. Failed transfer rolls back every row.
   8. A User cannot be Owner and member of the same Family.
   9. Cross-Family resource access is rejected.
5. Test the ten-Family limit with real concurrent transactions:
   1. A tenth Family succeeds.
   2. An eleventh Family fails.
   3. An invitation accepted at the limit fails.
   4. Two concurrent joins from nine total result in exactly ten.
6. Test invitation behavior:
   1. Exact username invitation.
   2. Exact User ID invitation.
   3. Both identifiers supplied fails validation.
   4. Neither supplied fails validation.
   5. No user-search route exists.
   6. Ineligible targets receive the same public response.
   7. Duplicate pending invitations are rejected.
   8. Acceptance creates a Member and deletes the invitation.
   9. Decline and cancellation delete the invitation.
   10. Expired invitations cannot be accepted.
7. Test grocery-list behavior:
   1. Add, edit, check, uncheck, reorder, and delete.
   2. Quantity parsing never passes through binary floating point.
   3. Money remains integer cents.
   4. `added_by_id` is stable.
   5. Last committed concurrent edit wins.
   6. Update after deletion returns not found.
   7. Every query enforces Family scope.
8. Test completion and history:
   1. Purchased items create immutable history and leave the current list.
   2. Kept items remain, become unchecked, clear temporary purchase fields, and do not enter history.
   3. Discarded items leave the list and do not enter history.
   4. Per-item override beats the default unchecked action.
   5. Zero purchased items create no empty history header.
   6. Header and purchased snapshots commit atomically.
   7. History retains item text, quantities, cents, original adder, and completer after source deletion.
   8. Concurrent completion attempts cannot create duplicate purchase histories.
   9. Completion racing a list mutation yields a deterministic result without partial loss.
9. Require complete branch coverage for permission, ownership, membership-limit, token-rotation, completion, and money-handling paths.

## 26. Test the clients and infrastructure

1. Test the mobile critical journey:
   1. Register.
   2. Verify email.
   3. Log in.
   4. Create a Family.
   5. Invite by exact identifier.
   6. Accept invitation.
   7. Add and edit list entries.
   8. Check purchased entries and enter quantity/cents.
   9. Complete with keep/discard choices.
   10. Read purchase history.
   11. Transfer ownership.
   12. Log out current and all sessions.
2. Test mobile cache behavior:
   1. Successful snapshot persistence.
   2. Offline app start.
   3. Correct last-checked time.
   4. Warning placement and accessibility.
   5. Mutation controls do not falsely report offline success.
   6. Reconnect refresh.
   7. Logout cache cleanup.
3. Test future web direct navigation and browser refresh for every dynamic route once implemented.
4. Test infrastructure behavior:
   1. AD discovery and index validation.
   2. E2 Micro is the only approved shape.
   3. VM receives public IPv4.
   4. SSH permits only `admin_ipv4_cidr`.
   5. Public application ports remain closed.
   6. Tunnel reaches loopback API.
   7. MySQL has no public endpoint.
   8. Authorized VM VNICs reach MySQL through the client NSG.
   9. Unapproved VNICs cannot reach MySQL.
   10. MySQL requires TLS and least-privilege credentials.
   11. Terraform backend locking operates correctly.
   12. The AMD64 artifact starts within E2 Micro resource limits.

## 27. Execute the implementation phases in order

1. Phase 0 — Freeze contracts and risks:
   1. Finalize this plan and reconcile `ARCHITECTURE.md` before behavior implementation.
   2. Record durable decisions as ADRs for authentication, ownership representation, purchase-only history, and infrastructure topology.
   3. Confirm OCI home-region availability of `MySQL.Free` and `VM.Standard.E2.1.Micro`.
   4. Confirm the selected domain is active in Cloudflare.
   5. Confirm the AWS account and target SES region.
   6. Freeze Snowflake epoch and node allocation.
   7. Freeze JSON, ID, error, quantity, cents, and timestamp conventions.
   8. Exit only when external dependencies and conventions have executable proofs or documented operator steps.
2. Phase 1 — Scaffold the repository:
   1. Initialize the approved root directories and independent Go modules.
   2. Create the OpenAPI source.
   3. Create shared lint/test commands.
   4. Add local MySQL Compose services.
   5. Add CI skeletons without deploying anything.
   6. Exit when every module builds and the empty validation pipeline is green.
3. Phase 2 — Prove infrastructure:
   1. Bootstrap the OCI state bucket.
   2. Configure the OCI backend file pattern.
   3. Build network, NSG, MySQL, compute, Tunnel, and DNS modules.
   4. Query and select the availability domain.
   5. Prove SSH is restricted to the configured `/32`.
   6. Prove an authorized VM can connect privately to MySQL over TLS.
   7. Prove no public API or database socket is exposed.
   8. Exit when Terraform and network security tests are green.
4. Phase 3 — Implement schema and backend foundations:
   1. Build the migration executable.
   2. Create current-scope SQL migrations.
   3. Implement configuration, Snowflake generation, clocks, request IDs, structured logging, database connection, transactions, and error mapping.
   4. Integrate `go-playground/validator` with custom username and struct-level validators.
   5. Implement `/health`.
   6. Exit when migrations apply to real MySQL and foundational tests are green.
5. Phase 4 — Implement accounts and sessions:
   1. Implement User, credential, account-token, session, and email-outbox repositories.
   2. Implement registration and direct User responses.
   3. Implement verification and resend.
   4. Integrate SES delivery.
   5. Implement login, 30-minute access JWTs, refresh rotation, current logout, logout-all, and password reset.
   6. Implement `/v1/users/me` reads, edits, password change, and deletion constraints.
   7. Exit when all authentication and session critical paths are green against real MySQL.
6. Phase 5 — Implement Families and hierarchy:
   1. Implement Family ownership and membership repositories.
   2. Implement the permission policy.
   3. Implement Family CRUD.
   4. Implement the ten-Family limit and concurrent test.
   5. Implement exact-identifier invitations.
   6. Implement accept, decline, cancel, expiry cleanup, role changes, leave, removal, and ownership transfer.
   7. Implement relevant audit events.
   8. Exit when the complete role and invariant matrix is green.
7. Phase 6 — Implement list, completion, and history:
   1. Implement current-list repositories and services.
   2. Implement add, edit, check, uncheck, reorder, and delete.
   3. Implement actual quantity and cents entry.
   4. Implement atomic completion and purchase-only history.
   5. Implement history listing and detail.
   6. Run concurrency tests around completion and list mutation.
   7. Exit when purchased/keep/discard behavior is fully covered and green.
8. Phase 7 — Implement the mobile application:
   1. Implement account and session flows.
   2. Implement Family chooser and hierarchy administration.
   3. Implement list planning and shopping UI.
   4. Implement completion choices and purchase history.
   5. Implement secure token storage.
   6. Implement read cache and offline warning.
   7. Run the complete critical-device journey.
   8. Exit when the mobile acceptance journey is green on supported platforms.
9. Phase 8 — Deploy the initial production system:
   1. Complete Ansible roles.
   2. Configure SES production sending.
   3. Build and sign/version the AMD64 binary artifact.
   4. Apply migrations.
   5. Deploy API and Tunnel services.
   6. Run external health, authentication, Family, and list smoke tests.
   7. Invite only the intended small initial audience.
   8. Exit when the system is reproducibly deployable from Terraform, Ansible, migrations, and a versioned artifact.
10. Phase 9 — Add the complete website:
    1. Create the Vite/TanStack Router application.
    2. Implement all approved authenticated routes and account routes.
    3. Reuse the generated client and shared platform-neutral packages.
    4. Deploy on Cloudflare Pages.
    5. Verify direct refresh and dynamic-route fallbacks.
    6. Exit when web users can perform every current account, Family, list, completion, and history action.

## 28. Preserve the future catalog and price-comparison direction

1. Start future catalog work only after the current product is stable and used.
2. Keep these future concepts distinct:
   1. Category, such as soap.
   2. Brand, such as Dove.
   3. Organization or manufacturer, such as a brand owner.
   4. Product family.
   5. Exact sellable variant with package size and optional GTIN.
   6. Retailer-specific listing.
   7. Physical supermarket branch.
   8. Time-stamped assortment assertion.
   9. Time-bounded offer and promotion.
3. Keep a freeform grocery-list name even after a catalog exists.
4. Resolve a freeform request to an exact purchased variant only when the shopper selects or scans one.
5. Model bottle or package size on the exact variant, not on a generic category.
6. Store future media in OCI Object Storage with provenance, licensing, content hashes, dimensions, and moderation metadata.
7. Never scrape or hotlink retailer images without contractual rights.
8. Integrate only one retailer provider first through a provider adapter and reviewed mapping table.
9. Match future provider products in this order:
   1. Exact validated GTIN.
   2. Existing reviewed provider mapping.
   3. Brand/name/package candidates requiring review.
   4. Manual mapping.
10. Never merge products automatically merely because their titles look similar.
11. Treat missing retailer data as unknown, not automatically out of stock.
12. Display source, branch, retrieval time, validity, promotion conditions, and freshness with every future comparison.
13. Add the HeatWave analytical accelerator only after a measured analytical query demonstrates value.
14. Add public product or price pages only when catalog quality and acquisition value justify revisiting public website routes.

## 29. Accept the system only when the current scope is complete

1. Verify account creation returns a direct User representation with string Snowflake IDs and `snake_case` JSON.
2. Verify all request DTOs use the shared validator and malformed JSON has no field map.
3. Verify email verification, login, refresh rotation, current logout, logout-all, and password reset.
4. Verify access JWTs last 30 minutes and contain no Family authorization.
5. Verify current database authorization takes effect immediately after member removal.
6. Verify a User cannot exceed ten owned/joined Families under concurrent operations.
7. Verify every Family has one Owner through `owner_id` and only Manager/Member membership roles.
8. Verify invitations require an exact username or ID and expose no user-discovery surface.
9. Verify the current list supports all planned mutations with last-write-wins behavior.
10. Verify completion stores only purchased items in history, keeps selected unchecked entries, and deletes discarded entries.
11. Verify item adder and purchase completer attribution survives in history.
12. Verify zero purchased items produce no empty history row.
13. Verify mobile cache and offline warning behavior.
14. Verify the API VM is E2 Micro, publicly addressable only for restricted SSH, and reached publicly for the API only through Cloudflare Tunnel.
15. Verify every authorized VM can access private MySQL through NSG membership and unauthorized VMs cannot.
16. Verify migrations are independent from the API and GORM never changes schema automatically.
17. Verify Terraform, Ansible, backend, migrations, mobile, and infrastructure checks are green.
18. Verify documentation matches the implemented routes, entities, permissions, infrastructure, limitations, and deferred scope.
